    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>App Ecosystem</strong></td>
      <td>Onyx Boox (Tile UI, limited)</td>
      <td>Custom minimal OS</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Onyx Boox has a significant advantage in one area: the SD card slot. XTEINK devices do not offer expandable storage, limiting how many EPUB files you can carry. For readers who maintain large personal EPUB libraries, the Picco's expandable storage could be a deciding factor.</p>

<p>However, XTEINK has the magnetic phone-attachment feature that made the category popular in the first place. If the Picco lacks this, it will need to compete on price, availability, and brand trust — areas where Onyx Boox has an established track record.</p>

<h2 id="epub-workflow">How to Load EPUBs on the BOOX Picco</h2>

<p>Although Onyx Boox has not detailed the file-transfer workflow for the Picco, the company's existing devices follow a predictable pattern. Here is how EPUB loading will likely work:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>USB Transfer:</strong> Connect the Picco to a computer via USB-C. The device appears as a mass storage drive. Drag and drop EPUB files into the "Books" or "Documents" folder. This is the most reliable method and works with any EPUB file — DRM-free or Calibre-processed.</li>
  <li><strong>Calibre Integration:</strong> Onyx Boox devices support <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre</a> for library management. You can use Calibre's "Send to Device" feature to transfer EPUB files, complete with metadata and cover images. Calibre also handles format conversion if you need to convert AZW3, PDF, or DOCX to EPUB before transferring.</li>
  <li><strong>SD Card Pre-Loading:</strong> With the SD card slot, you can load EPUB files directly onto the card using a card reader, insert the card into the Picco, and start reading immediately. This is ideal for readers who want to swap libraries between devices.</li>
  <li><strong>Wireless Transfer (likely):</strong> Onyx Boox typically includes Wi-Fi and cloud-sync options on its Android-based devices. The Picco's Tile UI may include a pared-down cloud sync feature, though this is not confirmed for the entry-level model.</li>
</ol>

<p>For EPUB files protected by Adobe DRM — common with library books from OverDrive or purchased EPUBs from Kobo and Google Play Books — you would need to authorize the Picco with an Adobe ID. Onyx Boox's Android-based readers typically support Adobe DRM via the built-in NeoReader app, but the Picco's custom Tile UI may not include full DRM support at launch.</p>

<p>If you need to convert EPUB files to a compatible format for the Picco, see our guide on <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">Calibre EPUB workflow</a> — the same principles apply to any E Ink device.</p>

<h2 id="pocket-reader-trend">The Pocket E-Reader Trend in 2026</h2>

<p>The arrival of the BOOX Picco reflects a broader shift in the e-reader market. After years of larger screens and more features, 2026 has become the year of <strong>small, focused, distraction-free readers</strong>.</p>

<p>XTEINK proved there is demand for a device that is literally pocket-sized — something you carry everywhere because it takes up no meaningful space. The company's viral growth on social media, driven by the magnetic phone-attachment concept, has shown that the "purse-sized" e-reader category is real and growing.</p>

<p>Onyx Boox entering this space validates the trend. The Picco, priced at $99-$129, would sit below the <a href="/blog/pocketbook-era-lite-open-ebook-reader-2026/">PocketBook Era Lite</a> ($199) and well below the <a href="/blog/best-ereader-2026-expert-roundup/">full-size Kobo and Kindle models</a> ($130-$400). It is a pure reading device by design — no web browser, no notifications, no app store. Just EPUBs and a front light.</p>

<p>For EPUB enthusiasts, this is good news. More devices supporting EPUB natively means fewer format barriers. The Picco joins a growing ecosystem of EPUB-first readers that do not require format conversion. If you are building a <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB library</a>, a pocket reader like the Picco makes it easy to carry your collection everywhere.</p>

<h2 id="who-should-buy">Who Should Buy the BOOX Picco?</h2>

<h3 id="digital-minimalist">The Digital Minimalist</h3>
<p>If you want to reduce phone screen time and replace doomscrolling with reading, the Picco offers a dedicated distraction-free device. No email, no social media, no notifications — just a E Ink screen and your EPUB library. At the expected sub-$130 price, it is an affordable commitment to intentional reading.</p>

<h3 id="commuter">The Commuter Reader</h3>
<p>A 3.97-inch reader fits in any pocket or small bag. Commuters who currently read on their phone will appreciate the E Ink display's lack of eye strain and the ability to read outdoors in direct sunlight. The front light covers low-light subway and bus rides.</p>

<h3 id="epub-enthusiast">The EPUB Enthusiast Moving Away from Phones</h3>
<p>If you maintain a curated EPUB library — downloaded from <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">Standard Ebooks, Project Gutenberg</a>, or purchased DRM-free — the Picco with its SD card slot is an ideal companion. You can carry your entire collection in a device smaller than a smartphone.</p>

<h2 id="challenges">Challenges and Unknowns</h2>

<p>Several important questions remain unanswered about the BOOX Picco:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Release date:</strong> Onyx Boox has teased the device but not announced a launch date. It could be weeks or months away.</li>
  <li><strong>Magnetic attachment:</strong> The key differentiator for XTEINK is the MagSafe-compatible magnetic backplate. It is unclear whether the Picco will offer this feature.</li>
  <li><strong>Battery life:</strong> Pocket-sized devices have less room for batteries. The Picco's battery capacity and expected reading time are unknown.</li>
  <li><strong>DRM support:</strong> If the custom Tile UI does not support Adobe DRM, library EPUBs and purchased DRM-protected ebooks may not work without conversion via Calibre.</li>
</ul>

<p>For readers who want a pocket EPUB reader today, the XTEINK X4 Pro is available now. But if you prefer Onyx Boox's ecosystem and want the expandable storage, the Picco is worth waiting for. As more details emerge, we will update this guide with confirmed specs, pricing, and EPUB compatibility information.</p>

